.\" Based on PR#2411
.\"
.Dd October 10, 2026
.Dt TUN 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tun
.Nd tunnel software network interface
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd pseudo-device tun
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
interface is a software loopback mechanism that can be loosely
described as the network interface analog of the
.Xr pty 4 ,
that is,
.Nm
does for network interfaces what the
.Xr pty 4
driver does for terminals.
.Pp
The
.Nm
driver, like the
.Xr pty 4
driver, provides two interfaces: an interface like the usual facility
it is simulating
(a network interface in the case of
.Nm ,
or a terminal for
.Xr pty 4 ) ,
and a character-special device
.Dq control
interface.
A client program transfers IP (by default) packets to or from the
.Nm
.Dq control
interface.
The
.Xr tap 4
interface provides similar functionality at the Ethernet layer:
a client will transfer Ethernet frames to or from a
.Xr tap 4
.Dq control
interface.
.Pp
The network interfaces are named
.Dq Li tun0 ,
.Dq Li tun1 ,
etc.
Each interface has one control device,
.Pa /dev/tun Ns Ar N .
Interfaces are created in one of two ways:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Executing Dq Cm ifconfig Ar tunN Cm create
This command creates interface
.Ar tunN
with
.Pa /dev/tunN
as its control device.
This is the preferred method.
The interface persists until it is removed with
.Dq Cm ifconfig Ar tunN Cm destroy
or until the
.Pa if_tun.ko
module is unloaded.
.It Opening Pa /dev/tun
Opening the auto-cloning device
.Pa /dev/tun
creates a new interface using the lowest unused unit number and returns a
handle to its control device (use
.Xr devname 3
to determine which).
Such an interface is automatically destroyed on the last close of its
control device.
.El
.Ss Access Control
Opening the auto-cloning device
.Pa /dev/tun
requires super-user privileges, since it creates a new interface.
.Pp
Access to the per-interface control devices
.Pa /dev/tun Ns Ar N
is governed by the file permissions of the device node,
which are created owned by
.Dq root:wheel
with mode
.Li 0600
by default.
To delegate an interface to an unprivileged user, the administrator creates
it and changes the ownership or mode of its device node, for example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ifconfig tun0 create
chown user /dev/tun0
.Ed
.Pp
The node is removed when the interface is destroyed and created afresh with
the default ownership the next time, so use
.Xr devfs 5
rules to make a permission change persistent.
The owner of a control device can read and write packets and change the
per-interface settings described below.
.Ss Operation
Each interface supports the usual network-interface
.Xr ioctl 2 Ns s
and thus can be used with
.Xr ifconfig 8
like any other interface.
By default, they are
.Dv POINTOPOINT
interfaces, but this can be changed; see the description of
.Dv TUNSIFMODE
below.
When the system chooses to transmit a packet on the
network interface, the packet can be read from the control device
(it appears as
.Dq input
there);
writing a packet to the control device generates an input
packet on the network interface, as if the (non-existent)
hardware had just received it.
.Pp
The control device is exclusive-open
(it cannot be opened if it is already open).
Packets output on the interface are queued for reading only while the
control device is open and the interface is up; otherwise they are
dropped.
.Pp
A
.Xr read 2
will return a packet if one is available;
if not, it will either block until one is or return
.Er EWOULDBLOCK ,
depending on whether non-blocking I/O has been enabled.
Each
.Xr read 2
returns exactly one packet.
If the packet is longer than is allowed for in the buffer passed to
.Xr read 2 ,
the extra data will be silently dropped.
.Pp
A
.Xr write 2
call passes a packet in to be
.Dq received
on the pseudo-interface.
Each
.Xr write 2
call supplies exactly one packet;
the packet length is determined from the amount of data provided to
.Xr write 2 .
Writes will not block; if the packet cannot be accepted for a
transient reason
(e.g., no buffer space available),
it is silently dropped; if the reason is not transient
(e.g., packet too large),
an error is returned.
.Pp
In both directions, the packet data may be preceded by a header,
depending on the mode of the control device as described in the
.Sx Modes
section.
.Pp
The control device also supports
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
and thereby
.Xr select 2
and
.Xr poll 2 ,
for read; selecting for write is pointless, and always succeeds, since
writes are always non-blocking.
.Pp
On the last close of the control device, the interface is
brought down
(as if with
.Dq Cm ifconfig Ar tunN Cm down ) ,
and all queued packets are thrown away.
An interface that was created by opening
.Pa /dev/tun
is then destroyed; one created with
.Xr ifconfig 8
remains and can be opened again.
.Ss Modes
The mode of the control device determines which header, if any, precedes
the packet data.
It is selected with the
.Dv TUNSIFHEAD
and
.Dv TUNSLMODE
ioctls, which are mutually exclusive: enabling one turns the other off.
.Bl -tag -width "link-layer"
.It default
When neither ioctl has been set, packets are exchanged as bare IP packets
without a header.
Since a packet then carries no address family, a written packet is assumed
to be of type
.Dv AF_INET ,
and only IPv4 packets are passed to the control device;
output packets of other address families are discarded.
.It multi-af
Selected by
.Dv TUNSIFHEAD .
Every packet is preceded by a 4-byte address family in network byte order.
Packets read from the control device carry the address family of the
packet.
Packets written must be prepended with the address family and must contain
at least one byte of data after it, otherwise
.Er EINVAL
is returned.
This is the mode to use to carry IPv6, or a mix of address families.
.It link-layer
Selected by
.Dv TUNSLMODE .
Packets read from the control device are prepended with the destination
address in
.Vt struct sockaddr
format; its actual length is in the member
.Va sa_len .
Only reads are affected: packets written carry no header and are handled
as in the
.Dq default
mode, i.e., they are assumed to be of type
.Dv AF_INET .
.El
.Ss Ioctl Requests
The following
.Xr ioctl 2
requests are supported
(defined in
.In net/tun/if_tun.h ) :
.Bl -tag -width ".Dv TUNSIFMODE"
.It Dv TUNSDEBUG
The argument should be a pointer to an
.Vt int ;
this sets the internal debugging variable to that value.
The variable is global: it applies to all
.Nm
devices, not only the one the request was made on, and it is the same
variable that is exposed as the
.Va net.link.tun.debug
sysctl.
.Pp
.Em Note :
This request requires super-user privileges.
.It Dv TUNGDEBUG
The argument should be a pointer to an
.Vt int ;
this stores the internal debugging variable's value into it.
.It Dv TUNSIFINFO
The argument should be a pointer to an
.Vt struct tuninfo
and allows setting the MTU and the baudrate of the tunnel device.
The type must match the type returned by
.Dv TUNGIFINFO ,
otherwise the call fails with
.Er EPROTOTYPE .
An MTU outside the range supported by the driver is rejected with
.Er EINVAL .
.Vt struct tuninfo
is declared in
.In net/tun/if_tun.h .
.It Dv TUNGIFINFO
The argument should be a pointer to an
.Vt struct tuninfo ,
where the current MTU, type, and baudrate will be stored.
.It Dv TUNGIFNAME
Retrieve the name of the network interface, which may be renamed,
that is associated with the control device.
The argument should be a pointer to a
.Va struct ifreq .
The interface name will be returned in the
.Va ifr_name
field.
.It Dv TUNSIFMODE
The argument should be a pointer to an
.Vt int ;
its value must be either
.Dv IFF_POINTOPOINT
or
.Dv IFF_BROADCAST
and should have
.Dv IFF_MULTICAST
OR'd into the value if multicast support is required.
The corresponding flags of the
.Dq Li tun Ns Ar N
interface are set accordingly.
If the value is anything else, an
.Er EINVAL
error is returned.
The interface must be down at the time; if it is up, an
.Er EBUSY
error is returned.
.It Dv TUNSLMODE
The argument should be a pointer to an
.Vt int ;
a non-zero value turns off
.Dq multi-af
mode and turns on
.Dq link-layer
mode; see
.Sx Modes .
.It Dv TUNSIFPID
Will set the PID owning the tunnel device to the current process's PID.
.It Dv TUNSIFHEAD
The argument should be a pointer to an
.Vt int ;
a non-zero value turns off
.Dq link-layer
mode, and enables
.Dq multi-af
mode; see
.Sx Modes .
.It Dv TUNGIFHEAD
The argument should be a pointer to an
.Vt int ;
the ioctl sets the value to one if the device is in
.Dq multi-af
mode, and zero otherwise.
.It Dv FIOASYNC
Turn asynchronous I/O for reads
(i.e., generation of
.Dv SIGIO
when data is available to be read)
off or on, according as the argument
.Vt int Ns 's
value is or is not zero.
.It Dv FIONREAD
If any packets are queued to be read, store the size of the first one
into the argument
.Vt int ;
otherwise, store zero.
.It Dv FIOSETOWN
Set the process or process group to receive
.Dv SIGIO
signals, when asynchronous I/O is enabled, to the argument
.Vt int
value.
A negative value denotes a process group.
.It Dv FIOGETOWN
Retrieve the process or process group value for
.Dv SIGIO
signals into the argument
.Vt int
value.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /dev/tun Ns Ar N" -compact
.It Pa /dev/tun
auto-cloning device, super-user only
.It Pa /dev/tun Ns Ar N
control device of interface
.Dq Li tun Ns Ar N ,
access governed by its file permissions
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr devname 3 ,
.Xr inet 4 ,
.Xr inet6 4 ,
.Xr tap 4 ,
.Xr devfs 5 ,
.Xr ifconfig 8
